  "slug": "dynamics-somatic-l1-19d0",
  "summary": "Seven preprints apply single-cell, long-read and k-mer methods to variation in retrotransposition, satellites, methylation, chromatin and karyotype.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is resolving genomic and epigenomic variability that bulk or assembly-based measurements obscure. LUSTRE maps L1 insertions in ~17,600 adult human neurons to estimate somatic retrotransposition rates, and k-mer tools size Yq12 satellite arrays across 1,597 males, including father-son dynamics and testis-expressed satellite RNA. Methodological items extend this to the epigenome: ChIMe-seq jointly profiles histone modifications, DNA methylation and nucleosome positioning in single molecules and cells; TrES-seq pairs scRNA-seq with nanobody-based histone mark profiling in human tonsil cells; COMET assigns bulk long-read reads to cell types by methylation, separating composition shifts from within-cell changes. Two studies characterize variability directly: genome folding differs between pluripotent and hepatocyte-like cells, and acral melanomas show continuous karyotypic and structural fluctuation linked to MMBIR repair. The systems are heterogeneous, so the shared element is the measurement strategy rather than a common biological mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
